{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1448263-attributes-and-skills-conceptual-reach-how-to-truly-grasp-their-limit","id":1448263,"title":"Attributes and skills conceptual reach: How to truly grasp their limit?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-05-11T15:21:16-07:00","id":1448263,"title":"Attributes and skills conceptual reach: How to truly grasp their limit?","content":"Hello everyone!<br /> <br /> I doubt this has probably been discussed already, but I could not find anything satisfactory on the subject.<br /> I\u2019m currently running a weekly campaign using the Chronicles of Darkness rules and I plan on jumping on Vampire the Requiem as soon as I\u2019m done with the current one.<br /> <br /> \u200bI have recently realised that the more I think about some of the attributes and skills, the less I can find myself really interpreting how they differs. Of course, the physical ones are obvious, but the social and mental one are the one that makes me question my whole grasp of their conceptual reach.<br /> <br /> Before I lay out my actual confusions, I will try to present how I understand each of the following attributes that puzzles me:<ul><li><b>Intelligence:</b> As an attribute of power, I view intelligence as the raw computing power of the brain. It is about memory, logic and knowledge. When I think of high intelligence, I think about doctors, math majors, Engineer, etc.</li> <li><b>Wits:</b> As an attribute of finesse, I view wits as a way of adapting, react, picking-up on cues. As the stats of both defense and perception, I also view wits as an \u2018\u2019awareness\u2019\u2019 kind of stats. When I think of high wits, I think about investigators, social workers, or survivalist.</li> <li><b>Resolve:</b> As an attribute of resistance, I view Resolve as your inner strength, your determination, and focus. When I think about high resolve, I think about those who persevere despite hardship. For example, single struggling parents that keep doing everything they can to keep their kids fed.</li> <li><b>Presence:</b> As an attribute of power, I see Presence as your raw charisma, your assertiveness, your ability to be heard. When I think of high presence, I think about famous politicians, Celebrities, Orator.</li> <li><b>Manipulation:</b> As an attribute of finesse, I view manipulation has an aptitude with words, as an ability to make others change their mind without realising it wasn\u2019t their idea and as a way of veiling your intentions. When I think of high manipulation, I think of skilled politician, car salesman, Lawyers.</li> <li><b>Composure:</b> As an attribute of resistance, I view composure has your ability to remain calm in stressful situation, has a way to keep a cool head with all hell is breaking lose. When I think of high composure, I think about movie heroes that always keep their cool, or the cool guy at school that can brush off any insult.</li> </ul>Now, this all seems fine and good, but the more I think about it, and how they interact with skills in general, I feel like those attributes are too general or broad and that them seems to be conceptual overlap which makes me confused as to which attribute, I should key of certain actions in some specific moment. Lets go over some cases that confused me:<ul><li><b>Intelligence Vs Wits:</b> This one I though I had a grasp on, until I read some example in the skill description section.<ul><li><b>Investigation:</b> investigating a scene keyed of Intelligence + Investigation, but that examining a scene and identifying patterns called a wits+investigation rolls. Wouldn\u2019t it be more appropriate to have \u2018\u2019investigating\u2019\u2019 a scene be based on wits instead?</li> </ul></li> <li><b>Presence vs Manipulation:</b> This one I\u2019ve always had a bit of trouble drawing true distinction. When I think about the definition, I can definitely see how they differ, but when I apply them to skills, the differences start to get muddied a bit. At lot of the time, it seems like a matter of \u2018\u2019approach\u2019\u2019 rather than an actual difference.<ul><li><b>For example:</b><ul><li><b>Intimidation:</b> The samples actions are clears and quite enlightening, a stare down calls for presence and subtle threat calls for manipulation but the interrogation action calls for manipulation instead of presence. Why? Wouldn\u2019t both be of any use in that case? Is this because I should have a police kind of interrogation in mind where they use dirty trick to make you talks? Wouldn\u2019t it also work by using presence to inspire fear? If \u2018\u2019Interrogation\u2019\u2019 just means getting information by tricking them, wouldn\u2019t it be better to ask for a subterfuge roll?</li> <li><b>Persuasion:</b> That skill in its own puzzle me, but I\u2019ll come back to it later. Why is seduction specifically manipulation, instead of presence? Why is an impassioned plea presence instead of manipulation?</li> <li><b>Socialize:</b> The action of \u2018\u2019Carousing\u2019\u2019 call for a Presence+ Socialize/Streetwise, but in Socialize, one of the sample actions that was given was \u2018\u2019carouse\u2019\u2019 and it calls for a manipulation + socialize roll.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> <li><b>Manipulation vs Composure:</b> The difference in those two attributes is quite star, but both seems to overstep each other when it comes to hiding emotions. Under attribute, the sample action for manipulation is \u2018\u2019poker face\u2019\u2019, but in the subterfuge skill, it call for a composure + manipulation rolls.</li> <li><b>Resolve vs Composure: </b>That one seems mostly fine to me, I just wanted to make sure I utterly understood the difference. Would it be right to say that resolve is your mental fortitude while composure is your emotional fortitude? That way when skills and power affect the way you think it gets resisted by your resolve, while skills and power that try to change the way you feel, it gets resisted by composure. Am I correct?`</li> </ul><br /> For my final confusion: Persuasion. It\u2019s clear, it knows what it should do, it\u2019s to make people change their mind. How is it then that, so many other skills seems to accomplish what it does?<ul><li><b>Expression:</b> The \u2018\u2019Argument\u2019\u2019 action, which call for an intelligence + Expression, can convince people of the truth (or apparent) of your words. Also, why does it keys intelligence and not manipulation?</li> <li><b>Subterfuge:</b> The \u2018\u2019Fast Talk\u2019\u2019 action calls for a manipulation + subterfuge roll, but it\u2019s a sample action of persuasion.</li> <li><b>Empathy: </b>Soothing someone\u2019s nerve can be achieved through empathy.</li> </ul>Am I alone in my confusion? Anyone willing to give me some guidance? I hope I\u2019m not being too picky on examples; I\u2019m really trying to get a firm sense of which attributes and skills should be used in given situation and I always feel like I\u2019m struggling. I\u2019d like to avoid solution that just end up letting anyone using any attributes they feel like whenever they can by changing a word or two as I feel it would encourage dumping stats over others. After all, why would I need presence, if I can almost always guarantee I can use manipulation to influence others? And vice versa.<br /> <br /> Well, this was way longer than I anticipated, so thank you for taking your time to read my wall of confusion!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-11T17:19:44-07:00","id":1448268,"title":"","content":"OK, so a big piece of general advice:<br /> <br /> Treat example rolls as illustrative, not prescriptive. There isn't a single correct Attribute + Skill roll for any given action. It's fine to say, &quot;well the book says to use Manipulation, but this situation feels like Presence to me,&quot; or to let players make a case for a different roll.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1448263-attributes-and-skills-conceptual-reach-how-to-truly-grasp-their-limit#post1448263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200bI have recently realised that the more I think about some of the attributes and skills, the less I can find myself really interpreting how they differs. Of course, the physical ones are obvious, but the social and mental one are the one that makes me question my whole grasp of their conceptual reach.</div> </div> </div> </div>Your general explanations seem to be on point to me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ]<b>Investigation:</b> investigating a scene keyed of Intelligence + Investigation, but that examining a scene and identifying patterns called a wits+investigation rolls. Wouldn\u2019t it be more appropriate to have \u2018\u2019investigating\u2019\u2019 a scene be based on wits instead? </div> </div> </div>Intelligence, as you note is processing. When CSI goes into a crime scene, they're not looking for things they think are clues. They're looking at everything, then they process everything, and find the clues in that process. It can be slow, and tedious, but it's thorough because answers need data. The detective that comes to the scene and rolls Wits is looking for a starting point. As you note, finding patterns. They're trying to shortcut the thorough investigation to match the pattern at the scene to the collection of patterns in their brain. They can't wait for CSI to finish up completely before they start doing anything. They need something to give them ideas about what to ask the neighbors. They need to think about what databases to search or request access to. Etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Presence vs Manipulation:</b> This one I\u2019ve always had a bit of trouble drawing true distinction. When I think about the definition, I can definitely see how they differ, but when I apply them to skills, the differences start to get muddied a bit. At lot of the time, it seems like a matter of \u2018\u2019approach\u2019\u2019 rather than an actual difference. </div> </div> </div>Pretty much. However much it helps.... everyone runs into this one on some level.<br /> <br /> So, second piece of advice: don't be afraid to make approach matter. If the players need to get past a locked door, there's lots of options. Strength + (Stamina/Brawl/Weaponry) to bust it down. Dexterity + Larceny to pick the lock, or + Crafts to just disassemble the door. Wits + (Composure/Investigation) to look for a hidden spare key. Manipulation + Subterfuge to convince the building super to open the door for you. But with the door there's obvious complications to consider. A broken door is obviously broken. Circumventing the lock might not be as obvious, but still leaves signs. Finding a spare key assumes there is one. The super is a witness that you've talked to and might tell people about you.<br /> <br /> There's no reason to avoid the same kind of thinking with social problems. Presence and Manipulation might both convince someone to do something, but Presence means they don't necessarily think it's a good idea, and Manipulation doesn't necessarily mean they believe doing it for you is a good idea. You obviously don't want to undercut your players by sabotaging their successes or anything, but convincing someone to join you in a robbery with Presence might run into trouble when things start going wrong and the accomplice gets spooked, while doing it with Manipulation might mean someone thinks they can toss you under the bus and take the loot if the chance present itself. As the ST, use the Attributes your players lean on to give consequences flavor. Either they'll enjoy how their manipulative bastard gets in trouble sometimes as smug snakes tend to, or they'll try out options with lower dice-pools if they think they might want different stakes to the action. Both results are good.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Intimidation:</b>...but the interrogation action calls for manipulation instead of presence. Why? </div> </div> </div>Reality more than fiction. Actual police interrogations are very much acts of manipulation. It's not some powerful move where the bad cops scares the suspect into answering, or the charisma of the good cop gets them to open up. Interrogations are calculated down to temperature of the room, what sort of food is made available, and all sorts of little details to sell people on the idea that the interrogator is your friend, and you should help your friend out. Quite a large number of civil rights activists think police interrogations (at least without a defense lawyer present) should be considered inadmissible evidence because of how utterly coercive they are.<br /> <br /> [quote][*]<b>Manipulation vs Composure:</b><br /> Ultimately, resistance attributes are reactive, and the others are proactive in how the books tend to use them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Resolve vs Composure: </b>That one seems mostly fine to me, I just wanted to make sure I utterly understood the difference. Would it be right to say that resolve is your mental fortitude while composure is your emotional fortitude? That way when skills and power affect the way you think it gets resisted by your resolve, while skills and power that try to change the way you feel, it gets resisted by composure. Am I correct?` </div> </div> </div>I'd say so. It can be a hard line to see sometimes, but it's the essential line to draw. But again, don't stress it. The game doesn't break if you call for Composure and maybe it should have been Resolve. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Expression:</b> The \u2018\u2019Argument\u2019\u2019 action, which call for an intelligence + Expression, can convince people of the truth (or apparent) of your words. Also, why does it keys intelligence and not manipulation? </div> </div> </div>Argument seem to be meant to be more formal. Winning a debate, making a case in court to the judge, and so on. The kind of thing where being right is ultimately more important than clever turns of phrase.<br /> <br /> <br /> So yeah, your confusion is understandable because the game wants to be flexible here, which means solid lines are hard to find. Don't worry too much, even consider talking to your players. Do they want to be able to have dumpstats, or want to have good reasons to spread out? That's more important than threading a needle the game doesn't really worry about. I think you'll also find that once you get into supernaturals, some of these problems get addressed through a far more controlling factor: what powers make you roll. Power rolls are, generally, not negotiable. If you want to be good at Dominate you get good at Intelligence.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-11T17:38:06-07:00","id":1448270,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1448263-attributes-and-skills-conceptual-reach-how-to-truly-grasp-their-limit#post1448263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hello everyone!<ul><li><b>Intelligence Vs Wits:</b> This one I though I had a grasp on, until I read some example in the skill description section.<ul><li><b>Investigation:</b> investigating a scene keyed of Intelligence + Investigation, but that examining a scene and identifying patterns called a wits+investigation rolls. Wouldn\u2019t it be more appropriate to have \u2018\u2019investigating\u2019\u2019 a scene be based on wits instead?</li> </ul></li> </ul><br /></div> </div> </div> </div>When you're investigating a scene you're not looking for the single clue that cracks the case!, instead you're looking to reconstruct the entirety of the course of events using a mix of deductive and inductive reasoning.<br /> <br /> If the PCs are just scanning the area, then yes, Wits + Investigation makes sense. Likewise, if the PCs are sitting down and running data analysis on a pattern then that should be Intelligence + Investigation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <ul><li><b>Presence vs Manipulation:</b> This one I\u2019ve always had a bit of trouble drawing true distinction. When I think about the definition, I can definitely see how they differ, but when I apply them to skills, the differences start to get muddied a bit. At lot of the time, it seems like a matter of \u2018\u2019approach\u2019\u2019 rather than an actual difference.<ul><li><b>For example:</b><ul><li><b>Intimidation:</b> The samples actions are clears and quite enlightening, a stare down calls for presence and subtle threat calls for manipulation but the interrogation action calls for manipulation instead of presence. Why? Wouldn\u2019t both be of any use in that case? Is this because I should have a police kind of interrogation in mind where they use dirty trick to make you talks? Wouldn\u2019t it also work by using presence to inspire fear? If \u2018\u2019Interrogation\u2019\u2019 just means getting information by tricking them, wouldn\u2019t it be better to ask for a subterfuge roll?</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> </ul><br /> </div> </div> </div>Purely fear based interrogations get you the answer the person thinks you want, not the real answer, it's the same reason physical torture doesn't work: the person is in a situation where they'll say anything to get out of duress.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <ul><li><b>Persuasion:</b> That skill in its own puzzle me, but I\u2019ll come back to it later. Why is seduction specifically manipulation, instead of presence? Why is an impassioned plea presence instead of manipulation?</li> </ul> </div> </div> </div>Seduction is generally a more subtle thing, you're not simply going up to someone and whipping out your assets, it's more teasing and enticing. Basically, seduction is about finesse, not power.<br /> <br /> Conversely, an impassioned plea is nothing <i>but</i> power, you're laying it all on the table, there's no strategy, no subtly, no <i>manipulation</i>, it's guileless and forthright.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <ul><li><b>Socialize:</b> The action of \u2018\u2019Carousing\u2019\u2019 call for a Presence+ Socialize/Streetwise, but in Socialize, one of the sample actions that was given was \u2018\u2019carouse\u2019\u2019 and it calls for a manipulation + socialize roll.</li> </ul> </div> </div> </div>That one might be a mistake, carouse should probably be Presence.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <ul><li><b>Manipulation vs Composure:</b> The difference in those two attributes is quite star, but both seems to overstep each other when it comes to hiding emotions. Under attribute, the sample action for manipulation is \u2018\u2019poker face\u2019\u2019, but in the subterfuge skill, it call for a composure + manipulation rolls.</li> </ul> </div> </div> </div>Generally in skill rolls you would roll either Power or Finesse + Skill on &quot;offense&quot; and Resistance + Skill on &quot;defense.&quot; If you're trying to bluff someone with your poker face that's Manipulation, if you're trying to contest or resist a Wits + Empathy roll to tell that you're bluffing that's Composure.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <ul><li><b>Resolve vs Composure: </b>That one seems mostly fine to me, I just wanted to make sure I utterly understood the difference. Would it be right to say that resolve is your mental fortitude while composure is your emotional fortitude? That way when skills and power affect the way you think it gets resisted by your resolve, while skills and power that try to change the way you feel, it gets resisted by composure. Am I correct?`</li> </ul> </div> </div> </div>Pretty much, I think possession is also resisted by Composure.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For my final confusion: Persuasion. It\u2019s clear, it knows what it should do, it\u2019s to make people change their mind. How is it then that, so many other skills seems to accomplish what it does?<ul><li><b>Expression:</b> The \u2018\u2019Argument\u2019\u2019 action, which call for an intelligence + Expression, can convince people of the truth (or apparent) of your words. Also, why does it keys intelligence and not manipulation?</li> </ul> </div> </div> </div>Laying out a good argument is an act of logic, but only works on people on whom logic would work, if they're too emotionally invested then an argument, no matter how good, is unlikely to work.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <ul><li><b>Subterfuge:</b> The \u2018\u2019Fast Talk\u2019\u2019 action calls for a manipulation + subterfuge roll, but it\u2019s a sample action of persuasion.</li> </ul> </div> </div> </div>Because &quot;fast talk&quot; can be two different things. Manipulation + Subterfuge &quot;fast talk&quot; would be to trick someone, like to convince them a total lemon is a good deal; Presence or Manipulation + Persuasion &quot;fast talk&quot; would be to bypass their logic brain and go straight for their emotions without giving them a chance to realize that's what you're doing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <ul><li><b>Empathy: </b>Soothing someone\u2019s nerve can be achieved through empathy.</li> </ul> </div> </div> </div>That's calming someone down, not persuading them to change their view or do something for you; you're connecting with them, showing you're on their side, understanding what they're feeling, and helping ground them out, not putting on the charm and schmoozing them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Am I alone in my confusion? Anyone willing to give me some guidance? I hope I\u2019m not being too picky on examples; I\u2019m really trying to get a firm sense of which attributes and skills should be used in given situation and I always feel like I\u2019m struggling. I\u2019d like to avoid solution that just end up letting anyone using any attributes they feel like whenever they can by changing a word or two as I feel it would encourage dumping stats over others. After all, why would I need presence, if I can almost always guarantee I can use manipulation to influence others? And vice versa.<br /> <br /> Well, this was way longer than I anticipated, so thank you for taking your time to read my wall of confusion! </div> </div> </div>Hope my answers help!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1452235-proin-s-legacy-hub\" target=\"_blank\">proin's Legacy hub</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2757-proindrakenzol","name":"proindrakenzol","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1514"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-11T17:53:57-07:00","id":1448272,"title":"","content":"The difference might come from the difference from sample actions for Skills (where you try to leverage using a Skill in creative ways), and sample Common Actions (where they give out predetermined examples of what may be best used, in a <i>given</i> context)<br /> <br /> Anyways, my intuition is...<br /> <br /> <b>Investigation</b>: The sample common action of &quot;Investigating (or Tidying up) a Scene&quot; seems to imply you're taking time to logically working out what traces the culprit might have left, or what traces you might have left that investigators can look for. You have to <i>simulate </i>behaviors and information, thus, Intelligence. Both of the sample actions for the Investigation Skills seem to imply you're trying to <i>perceive </i>things that are already there, as opposed to creating simulations in your head. Thus, Wits.<br /> <br /> <b>Intimidation</b>: Reading the &quot;Interrogation&quot; common action, I do feel that yes this <i>is</i> using subtle threats. Using Presence to stare down someone looks more like a dominance/power play; good for making people shut up and look away, as opposed to spilling the beans.<br /> Subterfuge is probably reserved for con artists trying to weasel out private information out of you.<br /> <br /> <b>Socialize</b>: It feels like the difference between making the group as a whole like you and then using that likable-ness to approach someone in that group (Presence), and specifically circling around and gunning for an individual (Manipulation).<br /> <br /> <b>Concealing emotions</b> is Composure + Subterfuge, yes (I think you mistyped it as Composure+Manipulation?). However, <b>poker face</b> is exactly that; you're deceiving and tricking someone with guile, ie. Manipulation. It's a different context.<br /> <br /> <b>Resolve and Composure</b>, yeah they do look like that. Composure seems to have a bigger role in resisting general &quot;change the situation I am in&quot; rolls, though, such as for resisting curses.<br /> <br /> <b>Persuasion &amp;</b><br /> - <b>Expression</b>: The &quot;Argument&quot; common action sees you using logic and rhetoric, not just trying to leverage your personal charisma or way-with-words. It's a calculated formation of learned words and speech. Thus, Intelligence and Expression.<br /> - <b>Subterfuge</b>: Sometimes, you appeal to charm. Sometimes, you just lie your way out. The latter would call for Subterfuge instead of Persuasion, and that specific context seems to be what the Common Action &quot;Fast Talk&quot; seems to be describing.<br /> - <b>Empathy</b>: Changing what they want to do and changing how they feel do seem like different things.<br /> <br /> <br /> [Mandatory disclaimer of how all the above is just my thought on the matter and anyone is free to have alternate interpretations goes here]","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-11T18:04:33-07:00","id":1448275,"title":"","content":"Woah, thanks a lot, your clarifications are really appreciated! <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong> Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Treat example rolls as illustrative, not prescriptive. There isn't a single correct Attribute + Skill roll for any given action. It's fine to say, &quot;well the book says to use Manipulation, but this situation feels like Presence to me,&quot; or to let players make a case for a different roll.</div> </div> </div> </div>I had a feeling that this was something the game intended, but having it worded out, do makes it clearer, thanks.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong> Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Intelligence, as you note is processing. When CSI goes into a crime scene, they're not looking for things they think are clues. They're looking at everything, then they process everything, and find the clues in that process. It can be slow, and tedious, but it's thorough because answers need data. The detective that comes to the scene and rolls Wits is looking for a starting point. As you note, finding patterns. They're trying to shortcut the thorough investigation to match the pattern at the scene to the collection of patterns in their brain. They can't wait for CSI to finish up completely before they start doing anything. They need something to give them ideas about what to ask the neighbors. They need to think about what databases to search or request access to. Etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>This makes senses, yeah, i do feel like i might get confused again in the future, but it does clarify it. It's probably cause i don't have an habit of handling out the solution to a problem with an intelligence roll, generally, how investigations works in my game, is that rolls gives clues and hints and players pieces out the informations together solving the mystery. I do give some hints on intelligence roll though.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong> Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Pretty much. However much it helps.... everyone runs into this one on some level.<br /> <br /> So, second piece of advice: don't be afraid to make approach matter. If the players need to get past a locked door, there's lots of options. Strength + (Stamina/Brawl/Weaponry) to bust it down. Dexterity + Larceny to pick the lock, or + Crafts to just disassemble the door. Wits + (Composure/Investigation) to look for a hidden spare key. Manipulation + Subterfuge to convince the building super to open the door for you. But with the door there's obvious complications to consider. A broken door is obviously broken. Circumventing the lock might not be as obvious, but still leaves signs. Finding a spare key assumes there is one. The super is a witness that you've talked to and might tell people about you.<br /> <br /> There's no reason to avoid the same kind of thinking with social problems. Presence and Manipulation might both convince someone to do something, but Presence means they don't<br /> necessarily think it's a good idea, and Manipulation doesn't necessarily mean they believe doing it for you is a good idea. You obviously don't want to undercut your players by sabotaging their<br /> successes or anything, but convincing someone to join you in a robbery with Presence might run into trouble when things start going wrong and the accomplice gets spooked, while doing it with<br /> Manipulation might mean someone thinks they can toss you under the bus and take the loot if the chance present itself. As the ST, use the Attributes your players lean on to give consequences<br /> flavor. Either they'll enjoy how their manipulative bastard gets in trouble sometimes as smug snakes tend to, or they'll try out options with lower dice-pools if they think they might want different<br /> stakes to the action. Both results are good.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's amazing advice thanks! The consequences of social attributes or skills is something i didn't delve in too deep previously, outside of the obvious, but those extra nuances are very welcome! I'f i understand properly, presence mostly affect moods while manipulation affects mostly the mind?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong> Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Argument seem to be meant to be more formal. Winning a debate, making a case in court to the judge, and so on. The kind of thing where being right is ultimately more important than clever turns of phrase.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that's how i feel it should be used, although the book does mention ''... to try sway someone with a rational argument.'' , which i interpret as something more than just winning an argument. Although, as the ST, i feel like i'll probably limit the influencial power of intelligence, as i feel being intelligent and being able to sway people over with your arguments has little correletion. I know well from my philosophical background that smart people sometimes know not how to communicate and that well spoken people can win you over with something that sounds good even if it's not, if you're not careful. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong> Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Don't worry too much, even consider talking to your players. Do they want to be able to have dumpstats, or want to have good reasons to spread out?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, i do talk with my players and while i do stress that having a more balanced approach is something i encourage ''realistic'' character, the dumpstat approach most likely comes from our long background of DnD and from the aversion to failing that some player holds. I guess i can take part of the blame here, while i don't feel like i'm overtly punitive, some players like to try incredibly reckless stuff that has gotten a few of their characters killed in the past. So maybe it's a mix of fear of failling and wanting to be the ''best'' as some very specified task. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong> Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I think you'll also find that once you get into supernaturals, some of these problems get addressed through a far more controlling factor: what powers make you roll. Power rolls are, generally, not negotiable. If you want to be good at Dominate you get good at Intelligence.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that's true and some of my players even voiced some complains over having to invest in different skills and attributes for their future vampire characters. I personally like that disciplines requires different skills and attribute. :^) <br /> <br /> Again, thank you very much your response definitely helped!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-11T18:56:40-07:00","id":1448282,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Proindrakenzol</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> When you're investigating a scene you're not looking for the single clue that cracks the case!, instead you're looking to reconstruct the entirety of the course of events using a mix of deductive and inductive reasoning.<br /> <br /> If the PCs are just scanning the area, then yes, Wits + Investigation makes sense. Likewise, if the PCs are sitting down and running data analysis on a pattern then that should be Intelligence + Investigation.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah that makes more sense to me now indeed. It's pretty much how i played it out already, but after i reread the book it got me confused, thanks for clearing that up!<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Proindrakenzol</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Purely fear based interrogations get you the answer the person thinks you want, not the real answer, it's the same reason physical torture doesn't work: the person is in a situation where they'll say anything to get out of duress.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's... actually true, i'll keep that in mind for the future! <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Proindrakenzol</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Seduction is generally a more subtle thing, you're not simply going up to someone and whipping out your assets, it's more teasing and enticing. Basically, seduction is about finesse, not power.<br /> <br /> Conversely, an impassioned plea is nothing <i>but</i> power, you're laying it all on the table, there's no strategy, no subtly, no <i>manipulation</i>, it's guileless and forthright.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm... Fair. I guess presence could be used for a good ''first impression'' to get better chance at opening doors during social challenge.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Proindrakenzol</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Generally in skill rolls you would roll either Power or Finesse + Skill on &quot;offense&quot; and Resistance + Skill on &quot;defense.&quot; If you're trying to bluff someone with your poker face that's Manipulation, if you're trying to contest or resist a Wits + Empathy roll to tell that you're bluffing that's Composure.</div> </div> </div> </div>That makes senses with the game mechanics yeah. Although, i guess a contested action of bluffing with manipulation+subterfuge vs wits+empathy could also be called in some circumstances. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Proindrakenzol</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Laying out a good argument is an act of logic, but only works on people on whom logic would work, if they're too emotionally invested then an argument, no matter how good, is unlikely to work.</div> </div> </div> </div>That one is probably the only thing i kinda disagree. Laying out a good argument is a matter of communication skills, finding a good argument is more an act of logic. As a philosophy Bachelor, i've seen enough logicaly sound argument that are so poorly explained that they leave the interlocutor more confused that convinced. Sure a logical argument could win a debate in an academical context, or convince other researcher in a similar field that what they say is true, but that's mostly because it shows them the result of logic rather than the words being convincing by themselves.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Proindrakenzol</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because &quot;fast talk&quot; can be two different things. Manipulation + Subterfuge &quot;fast talk&quot; would be to trick someone, like to convince them a total lemon is a good deal; Presence or Manipulation + Persuasion &quot;fast talk&quot; would be to bypass their logic brain and go straight for their emotions without giving them a chance to realize that's what you're doing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ahhh... that explains my confusion then. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Proindrakenzol</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> That's calming someone down, not persuading them to change their view or do something for you; you're connecting with them, showing you're on their side, understanding what they're feeling, and helping ground them out, not putting on the charm and schmoozing them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah that's true, i guess persuarding someone to calm down would be more akin to trying to reason someone out of being angry with arguments. Which is generally a bad idea as it leave people feeling unjustified in their feelings. And it doesn't mean that it change their world view either, so it makes sense.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Proindrakenzol</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Hope my answers help!</div> </div> </div> </div>Definitely, thank you for your help!<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> <b>Investigation</b>: The sample common action of &quot;Investigating (or Tidying up) a Scene&quot; seems to imply you're taking time to logically working out what traces the culprit might have left, or what traces you might have left that investigators can look for. You have to <i>simulate </i>behaviors and information, thus, Intelligence. Both of the sample actions for the Investigation Skills seem to imply you're trying to <i>perceive </i>things that are already there, as opposed to creating simulations in your head. Thus, Wits.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, others as pointed this out as well, it makes more sense to me now, thanks!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> <b>Intimidation</b>: Reading the &quot;Interrogation&quot; common action, I do feel that yes this <i>is</i> using subtle threats. Using Presence to stare down someone looks more like a dominance/power play; good for making people shut up and look away, as opposed to spilling the beans.<br /> Subterfuge is probably reserved for con artists trying to weasel out private information out of you.</div> </div> </div> </div>This makes sense yeah, as others has pointed out, maybe i've watched too much movie lol. I guess someone scary just looking at you might get some word out, but they won't get the whole truth either. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> <b>Socialize</b>: It feels like the difference between making the group as a whole like you and then using that likable-ness to approach someone in that group (Presence), and specifically circling around and gunning for an individual (Manipulation).</div> </div> </div> </div>I guess it makes sense. At least to me it does. It does seems like presence is more of a group attribute while manipulation is more of a ''face to face'' attribute. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> <b>Concealing emotions</b> is Composure + Subterfuge, yes (I think you mistyped it as Composure+Manipulation?). However, <b>poker face</b> is exactly that; you're deceiving and tricking someone with guile, ie. Manipulation. It's a different context.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oops, you're right, i definitely meant composure+subterfuge. Well, isn't putting a poker face exactly conceilling emotion? I guess it's just a case in which characters will use the attribute they are the most proficient with. It kinda makes sense that someone that can alter his body language well is has able to put a poker face than someone who's unfazed and thus keeps a poker face.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">- <b>Expression</b>: The &quot;Argument&quot; common action sees you using logic and rhetoric, not just trying to leverage your personal charisma or way-with-words. It's a calculated formation of learned words and speech. Thus, Intelligence and Expression.</div> </div> </div> </div>This makes sense, but under expression ''reciting a speech'' is keyed of ''presence+expression. I feel like reciting an argument would likely be ''manipulation+expression'' or something like that. As i said previously during my philosophy degree i've read so many good but poorly layed out argument that i have hard time believe ''intelligence'', as understood in chronicles of darkness, can be used to influence someone. The intelligence part comes in finding the argument or the logic, not convincing people that it's true. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">- <b>Subterfuge</b>: Sometimes, you appeal to charm. Sometimes, you just lie your way out. The latter would call for Subterfuge instead of Persuasion, and that specific context seems to be what the Common Action &quot;Fast Talk&quot; seems to be describing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, i realise it now. I'll blame my non-perfect understanding of the english language. :^) <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">- <b>Empathy</b>: Changing what they want to do and changing how they feel do seem like different things.</div> </div> </div> </div>Although it's generally linked, you're right. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">[Mandatory disclaimer of how all the above is just my thought on the matter and anyone is free to have alternate interpretations goes here]</div> </div> </div> </div>Still very useful, thank you! <br /> <br /> Sorry for the double post, but i wanted to respond to you two guys as i was typing my previous message when you guys responded! Again, i appreciate the help!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-12T23:28:05-07:00","id":1448393,"title":"","content":"I think it is important to remember that like...rulebooks are really just instructions for how to make the game happen at your table. What <i>really</i> matters is that the group is grooving and arguments over &quot;which attribute&quot; don't drag things down.<br /> <br /> So far, far more important than whatever the book might say is that your group be in rough accordance during play. I suspect every single group will draw the lines between attributes slightly different places, and that's <i>fine</i>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2007-wade-l","name":"Wade L","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2007_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"136"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-13T05:59:17-07:00","id":1448412,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wade L</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1448263-attributes-and-skills-conceptual-reach-how-to-truly-grasp-their-limit?p=1448393#post1448393\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think it is important to remember that like...rulebooks are really just instructions for how to make the game happen at your table. What <i>really</i> matters is that the group is grooving and arguments over &quot;which attribute&quot; don't drag things down.<br /> <br /> So far, far more important than whatever the book might say is that your group be in rough accordance during play. I suspect every single group will draw the lines between attributes slightly different places, and that's <i>fine</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yeah and that's a fair point to make. Ultimately what matters is the flow of the game and the fun we have throwing dices around. I guess i'm just a bit more used to rigid rule as i have a long background of DnD. Although i do welcome the more open ended interpretation, as it's genuinely fun. I'll keep in mind Heavy Arms's ''difference of outcome'' way of differiciating attributes, i do like that a lot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-13T10:32:02-07:00","id":1448436,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1448263-attributes-and-skills-conceptual-reach-how-to-truly-grasp-their-limit?p=1448412#post1448412\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Yeah and that's a fair point to make. Ultimately what matters is the flow of the game and the fun we have throwing dices around. I guess i'm just a bit more used to rigid rule as i have a long background of DnD. Although i do welcome the more open ended interpretation, as it's genuinely fun. I'll keep in mind Heavy Arms's ''difference of outcome'' way of differiciating attributes, i do like that a lot.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Difference of outcome is definitely a good approach.<br /> <br /> But, yeah, overall... Chronicles of Darkness rules don't fit together as &quot;tightly&quot; as like D&amp;D 5E - nor are they nearly as well play-tested. I <i>prefer</i> CoD, but, like...the rules really are meant to be interpreted by humans and have the gaps filled in by the Storyteller's sensibilities. Coming from a game like D&amp;D, you really need to treat CoD rules as, well, &quot;less of a rule, more of a guideline&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2007-wade-l","name":"Wade L","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2007_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"136"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-14T09:52:13-07:00","id":1448517,"title":"","content":"In my experience, a lot times you're not really calling for a specific roll, but asking your players the kind of roll they want to use.<br /> <br /> As Heavy Arms said, different Attributes and Skills define the type of approach used, which is something that most of the time you let the players decide.<br /> One player may state a very general intention, like &quot;I want to interrogate the suspect&quot;, and the ST asks &quot;How do you proceed?&quot;.<br /> The more the player describes the specifics, the more it becomes clear the kind of roll that is called for. If the player imagines the interrogation as dangling the prisoner from a rooftop, Batman style, it may as well involve Strength instead of Manipulation.<br /> <br /> (P.S. Heavy Arms provided an excellent explanation as to why realistic interrogation may involve Manipulation. But my suggestion is, don't be afraid to use movie logic if realism may slow down the pace of the game)<br /> <br /> Also, some STs may feel like more or less generous when calling for rolls. If the character is untrained in the Skill you're calling, you can think of letting him approach the same problem from a different angle using a different Skill or Attribute. The character may not have Academics to know the whole history of the city, but she has enough Streetwise to know all its secret and forgotten places.<br /> <br /> The possibility of coming up with different combinations of Attribute + Skill is something I really love about the CofD system and one of its strong points IMO, and it's always fun to call for unusual combinations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26440,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26440-moonwhisper","user":"moonwhisper","date":". 05-14-2021, 01:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26440-moonwhisper","name":"moonwhisper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26440_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-14T11:35:36-07:00","id":1448526,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>moonwhisper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1448263-attributes-and-skills-conceptual-reach-how-to-truly-grasp-their-limit?p=1448517#post1448517\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(P.S. Heavy Arms provided an excellent explanation as to why realistic interrogation may involve Manipulation. But my suggestion is, don't be afraid to use movie logic if realism may slow down the pace of the game)</div> </div> </div> </div>I would say use TV/movie logic if it's better for the drama and the fun of the scene. Pacing is its own thing that can be handled in either method.<br /> <br /> I mean, the reason certain very unrealistic things get perpetuated in media is because the reality of it is... kinda dull The stereotypical police interrogation in a procedural show is there to tell a story, not replicate police tactics, so the interrogation includes all the aspects of a traditional mystery where the detectives unravel the case and evidence, and then do the big exposition of the truth and the bad guy confesses in the light of the obvious. It's just taking a Sherlock Holmes or Hercule Poirot narrative and shoving it into a police interrogation room (and even those are styled in a way that's easier to do dramatic cinematography in rather than what most really look like).<br /> <br /> If you want to have those story beats, screw reality.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-15T21:17:11-07:00","id":1448651,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>moonwhisper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1448263-attributes-and-skills-conceptual-reach-how-to-truly-grasp-their-limit?p=1448517#post1448517\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my experience, a lot times you're not really calling for a specific roll, but asking your players the kind of roll they want to use.<br /> <br /> As Heavy Arms said, different Attributes and Skills define the type of approach used, which is something that most of the time you let the players decide.<br /> One player may state a very general intention, like &quot;I want to interrogate the suspect&quot;, and the ST asks &quot;How do you proceed?&quot;.<br /> The more the player describes the specifics, the more it becomes clear the kind of roll that is called for. If the player imagines the interrogation as dangling the prisoner from a rooftop, Batman style, it may as well involve Strength instead of Manipulation.<br /> <br /> (P.S. Heavy Arms provided an excellent explanation as to why realistic interrogation may involve Manipulation. But my suggestion is, don't be afraid to use movie logic if realism may slow down the pace of the game)<br /> <br /> Also, some STs may feel like more or less generous when calling for rolls. If the character is untrained in the Skill you're calling, you can think of letting him approach the same problem from a different angle using a different Skill or Attribute. The character may not have Academics to know the whole history of the city, but she has enough Streetwise to know all its secret and forgotten places.<br /> <br /> The possibility of coming up with different combinations of Attribute + Skill is something I really love about the CofD system and one of its strong points IMO, and it's always fun to call for unusual combinations.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Lots of good points. As an ST i generally want my player to suceed, otherwise, well the chronicle wouldn't get really far. At first i was mostly worried because i felt like some attributes could be totaly ignored if most of them could be substituted for another one with a little bit of explanation. With the proposal to focus on outcome instead, it makes at lot more sense to me. <br /> <br /> As for using different skills, i generally allow it if it makes sense, or sometimes with penalty if i feel like the skill is a bit less apropriate. <br /> <br /> And yeah, i do like the more open-handed way of handling challenge in CofD, the main reason i decided to leave DnD for CofD was because i felt the game had a more natural flow to it. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would say use TV/movie logic if it's better for the drama and the fun of the scene. Pacing is its own thing that can be handled in either method.<br /> <br /> I mean, the reason certain very unrealistic things get perpetuated in media is because the reality of it is... kinda dull The stereotypical police interrogation in a procedural show is there to tell a story, not replicate police tactics, so the interrogation includes all the aspects of a traditional mystery where the detectives unravel the case and evidence, and then do the big exposition of the truth and the bad guy confesses in the light of the obvious. It's just taking a Sherlock Holmes or Hercule Poirot narrative and shoving it into a police interrogation room (and even those are styled in a way that's easier to do dramatic cinematography in rather than what most really look like).<br /> <br /> If you want to have those story beats, screw reality.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh yeah totally, i generally prefer to stay closer to some semblance of realism, but movie-inspiration can feel more appropriate sometime for narrative purposes. <br /> <br /> As for interrogation i think there's also the matter of strong interrogation being kinda illegal in most countries around the world. (Well at least, i would hope so). So it makes sense to base it on manipulation if you don't wanna rough your prisoner. And well, as you said, torture is not really effective either.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-15T22:18:42-07:00","id":1448656,"title":"","content":"To be fair, proindrakenzoi did more on the pointing out the ineffective nature of torture.<br /> <br /> While physical coercion (depending on how the law defines that) is illegal in most countries... that doesn't mean it's reality in most countries; esp. for minorities that are frequently subject to a harsher legal system than their fellow citizens despite a supposedly equal justice system.<br /> <br /> That said, &quot;not wanting to rough up your prisoner,&quot; is a consideration even if justice is more pretextual than it should be. Even show trials and kangaroo courts have trouble when it's clear the defendant has been severely physically abused.... since it ruins the idea of at least faking a neutral justice system for the public.<br /> <br /> Of course, the idea that &quot;strong&quot; interrogations simply result in the suspect telling the police what they want to hear is sometimes the exact point. Heavy handed interrogation tactics might be enough for probable cause or evidence for a search warrant application.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-16T07:23:08-07:00","id":1448684,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1448263-attributes-and-skills-conceptual-reach-how-to-truly-grasp-their-limit?p=1448656#post1448656\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To be fair, proindrakenzoi did more on the pointing out the ineffective nature of torture.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh my bad, too many people being helpful! :^)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1448263-attributes-and-skills-conceptual-reach-how-to-truly-grasp-their-limit?p=1448656#post1448656\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While physical coercion (depending on how the law defines that) is illegal in most countries... that doesn't mean it's reality in most countries; esp. for minorities that are frequently subject to a harsher legal system than their fellow citizens despite a supposedly equal justice system.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah unfortunately... <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1448263-attributes-and-skills-conceptual-reach-how-to-truly-grasp-their-limit?p=1448656#post1448656\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That said, &quot;not wanting to rough up your prisoner,&quot; is a consideration even if justice is more pretextual than it should be. Even show trials and kangaroo courts have trouble when it's clear the defendant has been severely physically abused.... since it ruins the idea of at least faking a neutral justice system for the public.<br /> <br /> Of course, the idea that &quot;strong&quot; interrogations simply result in the suspect telling the police what they want to hear is sometimes the exact point. Heavy handed interrogation tactics might be enough for probable cause or evidence for a search warrant application.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah also a good point, but this steps out of interrogation and steps into coercion territory. <br /> <br /> A use of interrogation with presence+intimidation to get information out of someone could also be limited to something quick to answer like ''Where's you boss?'', ''Who sent Who?'', ''What's the code for that door?'', etc. It would likely only be for as long as the threat of harm stays imminent.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}